---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";
import { FOODS } from "../data/foods";
import { breadcrumbLd } from "../lib/seo";

const jsonLd = [breadcrumbLd([{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology" }])];
---

<Base title={`Methodology & Food Safety — ${SITE.SITE_NAME}`} description="Where our air-fryer times and safe cooking temperatures come from, and how to use them safely." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "Methodology" }]} />
    <h1 class="text-balance text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Methodology & food safety</h1>
    <div class="prose mt-6">
      <p>
        We'd rather be honest and useful than pretend to a precision that air fryers
        don't allow. Here's exactly how {SITE.SITE_NAME}'s numbers work.
      </p>

      <h2>Two different kinds of numbers</h2>
      <p>Every food page has up to two numbers, and they come from very different places:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Safe internal temperature (authoritative).</strong> For meat,
          poultry, fish and eggs we show the <strong>USDA safe minimum internal
          temperature</strong> — e.g. 165°F for chicken, 160°F for ground beef,
          145°F for whole cuts and fish. These are published food-safety standards
          and are the real test of doneness. See the full
          <a href="/air-fryer-temperature-chart">temperature chart</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cook time & air-fryer temperature (guidance).</strong> These are
          practical starting points compiled from typical air-fryer results and
          manufacturer guidance, expressed as <strong>ranges</strong> because
          outcomes genuinely vary. They are not laboratory figures for your specific
          machine.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why times are ranges, not exact</h2>
      <p>Air fryers differ in ways that meaningfully change cooking:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Wattage & size</strong> — a compact 1,200 W basket and a large 1,700 W oven-style unit don't cook alike.</li>
        <li><strong>How full the basket is</strong> — crowding slows everything down and hurts crisping.</li>
        <li><strong>Starting temperature</strong> — straight from the fridge vs. room temperature vs. frozen.</li>
        <li><strong>Preference</strong> — "done" fries and "extra crispy" fries are minutes apart.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That's why we preset the built-in timer to the <em>shorter</em> end of each range: check early, then add time. It's the safe direction to err.</p>

      <h2>Food safety comes first</h2>
      <blockquote>
        Time is a guide. Internal temperature is the truth. For any meat, poultry,
        fish or egg dish, use a food thermometer and cook to the USDA safe
        temperature — regardless of what any timer says.
      </blockquote>
      <p>Undercooked poultry, ground meat and seafood can cause serious illness. When our time range and your thermometer disagree, the thermometer wins.</p>

      <h2>Updates & corrections</h2>
      <p>
        The dataset ({FOODS.length}+ foods) is hand-curated and versioned in our
        source repository, and validated on every build. If a time or temperature
        doesn't match your results, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> — real feedback
        makes the ranges better.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm"><em>{SITE.SITE_NAME} is an independent reference and is not affiliated with the USDA or any air fryer manufacturer.</em></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
